  "query_preview": "..Storm system has arrived a little bit earlier than\nanticipated with moisture ahead of the main trough generating some\nupslope rain showers along the Rim right now. As the day\nprogresses the West Coast trough will move eastward, but a lot of\nthe moisture and energy will move to the northeast as it does. \nThis means the western half of our CWA will see some good QPF at \n0.25\"-0.75\", the eastern half won't see much if anything with\namounts ranging only from 0 to 0.10\". Snow levels will start \npretty high this morning at around 9000ft but will begin to fall \nthis afternoon as the trough advances, getting down to 6500-7500ft\nby this evening, however by the time this happens most of the\nmoisture will have moved out of the area. So only the Kaibab\nPlateau and San Francisco Peaks will really see any accumulation,\nmainly above 8000 to 9000 feet. What likely will be the biggest\nimpact will be the strong wind brought with this system. Winds\nwill pick up this morning out of the southwest reaching 20-30 mph\nwith gusts 40-50 mph in places. A Wind Advisory goes into effect\nat 9am for most of Northern Arizona in areas north of the \nMogollon Rim as well as the mountains in the Prescott area, and\nremains in effect until 8pm.  \n\nAfter this storm gets out of here tonight things will dry out for\nWednesday and the first half of Thursday as another fast moving\nridge moves through. Temperatures won't recover much, remaining\nabout 5 degrees below average.  \n\nThe next trough moves in Thursday afternoon and each model\nrun has been going drier with a faster progression through the\nstate, with most of the system getting out of here now by late\nFriday afternoon. This is a colder system with snow levels\nstarting at 7000-8000ft and lowering to 6000-6500ft by the time it\ngets out of here Friday afternoon. So although this could be the\nfirst dusting of the year for the Flagstaff region, it will likely\nbe less than an inch, although the Kaibab Plateau where moisture\nis a bit more favorable could see 2 to 3 inches. Winds again will\npick up Thursday afternoon and remain gusty out of the southwest\nthrough Friday evening.  \n\nSaturday and Sunday at this time are looking dry, albeit cold with\ntemperatures 5 to 10 degrees below average. Some models are\nhinting at another storm system moving in Monday afternoon but\nwith each run showing something different, confidence remains low\nat this time on this scenario.",
